# What PBKDF2 holds, watched (kanso#2299).
#
# Each iteration is one HMAC: two compressions of one block from the key's
# kept pads. The loop that chains them hands the new HMAC and the running
# XOR to its next iteration, and nothing else an iteration builds is read
# again, so the arena should hold one iteration's work however many there
# are. A sign-in that stretches a password 600,000 times, as OWASP advises,
# is the case that matters.
#
# Watched, not frozen: regenerate deliberately and say which way it went.
import "std/sha256"
import "std/text"

pub play = print (sha256/pbkdf2 (text/bytes "pw") [1] 300 32)
